                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1381-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Sennheiser Electronics Corporation e 935</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Dynamic Microphone</h2>
                    <p>e 935 is a handheld cardioid dynamic for live vocal use. Along with the rest of the original e900 series, the e935 received a TEC award in 2005.</p><p>The mic features a metal body, internal shockmount, hum compensating coil and a moving-coil cartridge build with Neodymium rare-earth magnets for high output.</p><p>Its frequency response is rated at 40&ndash;18kHz, with lows rolled off below 150Hz and 2-3dB lift from 2-10kHz. In close-miked applications, the low end would be fuller due to proximity effect.</p><p>The wireless version, SKM 500-935 G3, is one of the most popular live vocal mics.</p><blockquote><p><cite>emusician, 2011</cite><br />When you get a mic capsule like the Sennheiser e935, the artist tends to be more true to their voice and perform better, because they are hearing better in their [in-ear monitors]. They are also not straining their voice as much and they are going for more emotive expression because they don&#8217;t have to fight the technology.</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Sennheiser/e-935">Sennheiser Electronics Corporation e 935</a> is also known as: e935, evo935.</p>
